<p>Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi’s Bharatiya Janata Party won control of West Bengal for the first time, defeating the Trinamool Congress in a state long held by opposition parties. Election Commission results cited by several reports showed the BJP winning 206 of 294 Assembly seats, ending Chief Minister Mamata Banerjee’s 15-year rule in the eastern state. Votes were counted on May 4 after elections across five Indian states and territories in April and May; West Bengal was the largest contest. Modi called the result a “record” win and said “people’s power has prevailed,” while Banerjee rejected the outcome, alleging the BJP had stolen “more than 100 seats” and calling it “an immoral victory”. Reports also described post-result clashes and a campaign dispute over the removal of millions of names from voter rolls.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/7sVCIdVSTfpjVLJiQFCH2oyL9G5GKTJvNBx4_QjIcbsWRURKlYrb_mS7BbI61pNj1V84taRxo91jjV0rn5LbMscwPZasG2CDSzS-21ERTeGh' alt='BJP supporters celebrate after taking a lead during vote counting for the West Bengal Legislative Assembly elections, near the party head office in Kolkata on May 4, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Other contests: The BJP retained Assam for a third consecutive term and remained in Puducherry’s ruling coalition, while a Congress-led alliance defeated Kerala’s Left Democratic Front.</li><li>Vote share: Analysts cited by Hum English said the BJP secured more than 44% of the West Bengal vote, up from about 39% previously.</li><li>Regional gains: The Hindu reported that the BJP held strongholds in north and southwest Bengal while making inroads in Kolkata and nearby districts.</li><li>Kerala foothold: RT reported that the BJP won three seats in Kerala for the first time, a rare southern gain for the party outside its traditional northern and western base.</li></ul><blockquote>The 2026 West Bengal Assembly Elections will be remembered forever - Narendra Modi</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><u